{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01QfLy8Q9Y8fMGQuG89gMyAb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tBOSTree -- An order statistic, self-balancing AVL tree implementation in C\n2\t\n3\tAn order statistic tree is a tree structure with two additional methods,\n4\trank(node) and select(index), which allow array-like access in O(log n) time.\n5\tThe idea is to simply store, for each node, the number of child-nodes.\n6\tSelf-balancing search trees maintain a minimal height by rebalancing their\n7\tsub-trees.\n8\t\n9\tI did not find a C reference implementation for such a tree, so I wrote one\n10\tmyself. This implementation uses map semantics, that is, stores key and value\n11\tas separate pointers.\n12\t\n13\tIt stores some not strictly necessary numbers, such as the number of sub-nodes\n14\tof the right child of a node. This allows to calculate the overall count\n15\tquickly, but can be removed to make the structure's overhead smaller.\n16\t\n17\tThe code has been extensively tested, is apparently AFL-proof, and leaks no\n18\tmemory whatsoever according to valgrind. BOSTree has been in use in pqiv for\n19\tsome time now.\n20\t\n21\t\n22\tALTERNATIVES:\n23\t\n24\t· In GNU/C++, Policy-Based Data Structures can be used according to\n25\t  http://stackoverflow.com/questions/11230734/c-stl-order-statistic-tree\n26\t  That code probably is much more mature. Use it if this is an option!\n27\t\n28\t· If you do not need the order statistic part, there are plenty of balanced\n29\t  tree implementations which suit your use case better. They are better tested\n30\t  and much faster because they do not have the overhead of order statistics. For\n31\t  example, see glib:\n32\t  https://developer.gnome.org/glib/stable/glib-Balanced-Binary-Trees.html\n33\t\n34\t· glib appears to have a concealed order statistic balanced binary tree\n35\t  implementation, only they name this sequences:\n36\t  https://developer.gnome.org/glib/stable/glib-Sequences.html\n37\t\n38\t\n39\tLicensed under the terms of the GPLv3.\n40\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e6f8ec78-fb9a-4c94-9423-23b2c4835260","uuid":"4297be38-06ea-4109-9a53-dbb11037178b","timestamp":"2026-05-29T09:20:43.110Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/harvest-crust-Gwr58w/project/c_src/README","content":"BOSTree -- An order statistic, self-balancing AVL tree implementation in C\n\nAn order statistic tree is a tree structure with two additional methods,\nrank(node) and select(index), which allow array-like access in O(log n) time.\nThe idea is to simply store, for each node, the number of child-nodes.\nSelf-balancing search trees maintain a minimal height by rebalancing their\nsub-trees.\n\nI did not find a C reference implementation for such a tree, so I wrote one\nmyself. This implementation uses map semantics, that is, stores key and value\nas separate pointers.\n\nIt stores some not strictly necessary numbers, such as the number of sub-nodes\nof the right child of a node. This allows to calculate the overall count\nquickly, but can be removed to make the structure's overhead smaller.\n\nThe code has been extensively tested, is apparently AFL-proof, and leaks no\nmemory whatsoever according to valgrind. BOSTree has been in use in pqiv for\nsome time now.\n\n\nALTERNATIVES:\n\n· In GNU/C++, Policy-Based Data Structures can be used according to\n  http://stackoverflow.com/questions/11230734/c-stl-order-statistic-tree\n  That code probably is much more mature. Use it if this is an option!\n\n· If you do not need the order statistic part, there are plenty of balanced\n  tree implementations which suit your use case better. They are better tested\n  and much faster because they do not have the overhead of order statistics. For\n  example, see glib:\n  https://developer.gnome.org/glib/stable/glib-Balanced-Binary-Trees.html\n\n· glib appears to have a concealed order statistic balanced binary tree\n  implementation, only they name this sequences:\n  https://developer.gnome.org/glib/stable/glib-Sequences.html\n\n\nLicensed under the terms of the GPLv3.\n","numLines":40,"startLine":1,"totalLines":40}}}
